\DOC CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV

\TYPE {CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV : conv}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves that values built by two different constructors of an inductive type
are different.

\KEYWORDS
conversion, inductive type.

\DESCRIBE
When applied to an equation {`C s1 ... sm = D t1 ... tn`} where {C} and {D}
are two different constructors of the same inductive type, the conversion
{CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV} returns the theorem:
{
   |- C s1 ... sm = D t1 ... tn <=> F
}
\noindent The arguments {s1}, ..., {tn} may be arbitrary terms, and the type
may be any instance of the type in question. This works for all types defined
by {define_type} and for the types {bool}, {num} (with the constructors {0}
and {SUC}), {list}, {option} and {sum}. The theorems are not obtained from the
distinctness theorem of the type (see {distinctness}), which has a conjunct for
each pair of constructors, but are proved, the first time a constructor is
used, from a theorem numbering the constructors, and then saved. Thus the
first call involving a constructor of a type with {n} constructors takes time
roughly proportional to {n}, and later calls are fast.

\FAILURE
Fails unless the term is an equation between applications of two different
constructors of an inductive type known to the system.

\EXAMPLE
Here is a type with three nullary constructors and one with arguments:
{
  # define_type "colour = Red | Green | Blue | Rgb num num num";;
  val it : thm * thm =
    (|- !P.
            P Red /\ P Green /\ P Blue /\ (!a0 a1 a2. P (Rgb a0 a1 a2))
            ==> (!x. P x),
     |- !f0 f1 f2 f3.
            ?fn.
                fn Red = f0 /\
                fn Green = f1 /\
                fn Blue = f2 /\
                (!a0 a1 a2. fn (Rgb a0 a1 a2) = f3 a0 a1 a2))
}
\noindent Equations between different constructors are proved false, for this
type and the built-in ones:
{
  # CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV `Rgb 1 2 3 = Blue`;;
  val it : thm = |- Rgb 1 2 3 = Blue <=> F

  # CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV `0 = SUC n`;;
  val it : thm = |- 0 = SUC n <=> F

  # CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV `INL (x:num) = INR (y:bool)`;;
  val it : thm = |- INL x = INR y <=> F
}
\noindent but the conversion fails if the constructors are the same, or if one
side is not a constructor application:
{
  # CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV `Rgb 1 2 3 = Rgb 1 2 4`;;
  Exception: Failure "CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV".

  # CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV `Red = c`;;
  Exception: Failure "CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV".
}
\noindent To use it on all such equations inside a term, apply it with a depth
conversion:
{
  # DEPTH_CONV CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV
      `~(Red = Blue) /\ (c = Green \/ Rgb 0 0 0 = Red)`;;
  val it : thm =
    |- ~(Red = Blue) /\ (c = Green \/ Rgb 0 0 0 = Red) <=>
       ~F /\ (c = Green \/ F)
}

\USES
Proving the distinctness of constructors for types with many constructors,
where the full distinctness theorem would be large.

\COMMENTS
The conversions used are the ones in {constructors_distinct_net}. The same
conversions are in {basic_rectype_net}, so for example {define} and
{MATCH_CONV} use them automatically.

\SEEALSO
basic_rectype_net, constructors_distinct_convs, constructors_distinct_net,
define_type, distinctness, injectivity, prove_constructors_distinct.

\ENDDOC
